{% extends "_layout.html.twig" %}
{% import "_makros.html.twig" as makros %}
{% block title %}Release process{% endblock %}
{% block content %}

    {%- include '_breadcrumb.html.twig' -%}

    <h1>Release process</h1>
    <p class="sds-lead">
        Releases are performed by project maintainers after the intended changes have been reviewed
        and merged. The process updates package metadata and the changelog, creates a Git tag and
        publishes the generated icon library to npm.
    </p>

    {%- include '_toc.html.twig' -%}

    {#- The rhythm between the sections, stated by the container Soul has
        for it. Left to the flow, the air above a heading is a rule on the
        block before it, and a block whose own margin ties with that rule
        wins on source order — a table before a heading closed the two to
        the step a paragraph gets. -#}
    <div class="sds-section">
        <h2>Before changing the version</h2>
        <p>
            Start from a clean, current default branch and confirm that the release contains only the
            changes intended for publication. Review new identifiers and aliases, check TYPO3 compatibility
            implications and run the complete build before selecting the version increment.
        </p>
        {{ makros.codeblock('npm ci
    npm run build', 'bash') }}
        <p>
            Use a patch release for compatible fixes, a minor release for compatible additions and a major
            release for breaking changes. The decision describes the public package contract, including
            identifiers and distributed file paths—not the amount of code changed.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="sds-section">
        <h2>Create the release commit and tag</h2>
        <p>
            The npm version lifecycle runs the configured preversion checks and changelog task. The project
            uses <code>[RELEASE] v%s</code> as its release commit message, and release commits are excluded
            from the generated changelog content.
        </p>
        {{ makros.codeblock('npm version [major | minor | patch]', 'bash') }}
        <p>
            Inspect the version change, changelog entry and generated metadata before pushing. The tag is
            part of the compatibility history, so correct mistakes before it becomes public.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="sds-section">
        <h2>Publish the release</h2>
        <p>
            Push the reviewed release commit and its tag. Do not push a release tag from an unreviewed
            feature branch.
        </p>
        {{ makros.codeblock('git push origin main --tags', 'bash') }}
        <p>
            The tag starts the release workflow. It first runs the same build as CI: stylelint, a fresh
            build of the icons and the check that the result matches the committed <code>dist</code>
            folder. Once the build has passed, the workflow checks that the tag matches the package version
            and publishes <code>@typo3/icons</code> to npm. It authenticates through npm trusted publishing, so no npm token is stored in the
            repository, and the package carries a provenance statement that links it to the workflow run.
        </p>
        <p>
            After the publish, the workflow creates the GitHub release for the tag. Its page lists the
            version's changelog entry, the install command and the commits since the previous release. A
            version with a prerelease suffix such as <code>-rc.1</code> is published to the
            <code>next</code> tag on npm and marked as a prerelease on GitHub. To read the release page
            before pushing the tag, run:
        </p>
        {{ makros.codeblock('scripts/release-notes.sh v<version>', 'bash') }}
    </div>
    <div class="sds-section">
        <h2>Verify the public result</h2>
        <p>
            Confirm that the publish workflow succeeded and that the npm package version, Git tag and
            release history agree. Check that the package
            contains the expected individual SVGs, sprites, CSS, Sass and data files, and verify that the
            documentation site still resolves the released identifiers and internal navigation.
        </p>
        <p>
            If a publication problem affects consumers, document it explicitly and follow with the smallest
            safe corrective release. Do not silently move or recreate a public Git tag.
        </p>
    </div>

{% endblock %}
